WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.161
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:38.161
[MUSIC PLAYING]
[Text:] Please tell us about yourself.

00:00:38.161 --> 00:00:39.969
[Shubin:] My name is Andrey Shubin.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:43.514
I was born in the Soviet Union,

00:00:43.514 --> 00:00:45.229
in Shevchenko, Kazakhstan.

00:00:45.229 --> 00:00:47.965
This city is now called Aktau.

00:00:47.965 --> 00:00:52.025
After the collapse of the Soviet Union,

00:00:52.025 --> 00:00:54.531
my parents and I moved to Russia.

00:00:54.531 --> 00:00:58.712
We lived in a small town in southern Russia
where I graduated from

00:00:58.712 --> 00:00:59.214
high school.

00:00:59.214 --> 00:01:01.907
After that, I entered the Moscow State University

00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:02.918
of Fine Chemical Technologies

00:01:02.918 --> 00:01:07.709
where I majored in biotechnology.

00:01:07.709 --> 00:01:10.616
After graduation, I entered the graduate school
of the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Academy

00:01:10.616 --> 00:01:11.116
of Sciences.

00:01:11.116 --> 00:01:14.447
I defended my thesis
and then worked for a short time

00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:19.478
at the Gamaleya Research Institute.
And then I came here

00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:22.185
where I took up a position
as a postdoctoral researcher

00:01:22.185 --> 00:01:24.826
at the Craig Hunter Laboratory
in the Department of Molecular

00:01:24.826 --> 00:01:26.640
and Cellular Biology
at Harvard University.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.363
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:39.363
[Text:] How did you choose your major,
and why did you choose biology?

00:01:39.363 --> 00:01:41.030
[Shubin:] Thank you,
that's quite an interesting question,

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:46.729
because all my life...
during my school years,

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:50.286
I didn't take biology seriously.

00:01:50.286 --> 00:01:54.646
I always wanted to be a chemist,
so I gave special attention to this discipline.

00:01:54.646 --> 00:01:56.195
I took extra classes

00:01:56.195 --> 00:01:59.163
in order to enter a good university
that offered a chemistry program.

00:01:59.163 --> 00:02:00.101
I succeeded.

00:02:00.101 --> 00:02:04.069
I vividly remember one episode.
I was demonstratively doing

00:02:04.069 --> 00:02:07.084
my chemistry homework
or solving some extra

00:02:07.084 --> 00:02:08.417
chemistry problems in a biology class.

00:02:08.417 --> 00:02:11.011
The biology teacher

00:02:11.011 --> 00:02:15.580
was also our homeroom teacher.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.465
I don't know if this system
even exists in American schools.

00:02:18.465 --> 00:02:21.386
I mean, this teacher supervises
a particular class.

00:02:21.386 --> 00:02:24.344
So, our homeroom teacher
was also our biology teacher.

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:26.916
I vividly remember her words.
She said,

00:02:26.916 --> 00:02:28.583
“Andrei, you should study biology,
it will come in handy for you”.

00:02:28.583 --> 00:02:29.855
I just...

00:02:29.855 --> 00:02:33.573
Her words amazed me.
I said,

00:02:33.573 --> 00:02:35.073
“How can biology come in handy for me?

00:02:35.073 --> 00:02:36.564
It's so boring”.

00:02:36.564 --> 00:02:38.439
However, things turned out
to be not as I expected.

00:02:38.439 --> 00:02:41.779
When I entered the institute...

00:02:41.779 --> 00:02:44.663
The thing is we had a major,

00:02:44.663 --> 00:02:46.714
yet we had some room for maneuver.

00:02:46.714 --> 00:02:48.264
The first four years...

00:02:48.264 --> 00:02:53.197
We could change our minds during the first 2 years
of our undergraduate studies.

00:02:53.197 --> 00:02:55.756
Needless to say,
I was so passionate about chemistry,

00:02:55.756 --> 00:03:00.662
and I was eager to get my hands on
some lab glassware as soon as possible

00:03:00.662 --> 00:03:02.079
and start synthesizing something.

00:03:02.079 --> 00:03:04.739
Therefore, I volunteered to work
at the laboratory

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:05.239
when I was a freshman.

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:08.212
However, I realized very quickly

00:03:08.212 --> 00:03:09.327
that it wasn’t my cup of tea.

00:03:09.327 --> 00:03:13.165
It was a real shock.

00:03:13.165 --> 00:03:18.997
And I am grateful to those people
who accepted me

00:03:18.997 --> 00:03:21.164
as the chemistry lab assistant,
because they gave me

00:03:21.164 --> 00:03:21.686
some very good advice.

00:03:21.686 --> 00:03:23.519
They told me,

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:27.880
“You know, we think molecular biology
might be more suitable

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:28.456
for you”.

00:03:28.456 --> 00:03:30.344
I said, “Well, let's give it a try”.

00:03:30.344 --> 00:03:33.251
So, when I was a sophomore,
I started working

00:03:33.251 --> 00:03:36.228
at the protein engineering lab
of the Institute of Molecular Genetics

00:03:36.228 --> 00:03:39.069
under the guidance of a brilliant researcher

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:44.362
Ilya Valerievich Demidyuk.
I worked with him for almost 10 years.

00:03:44.362 --> 00:03:47.898
I mean, we worked together
while I completed my bachelor's

00:03:47.898 --> 00:03:49.988
and master's degree programs,
as well as my postgraduate program.

00:03:49.988 --> 00:03:53.091
It is not uncommon for the Russian system.

00:03:53.091 --> 00:03:55.940
However, I know that in the United States

00:03:55.940 --> 00:04:00.766
you usually change the laboratory,
which may be more reasonable,

00:04:00.766 --> 00:04:01.641
but that's another story.

00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:04.146
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:04.146 --> 00:04:14.146
[Text:] What are you studying now?

00:04:14.146 --> 00:04:18.064
[Shubin:] At the end of my master's degree program
and during my postgraduate program,

00:04:18.064 --> 00:04:22.430
I really wanted to become a biologist

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:25.190
and work with the whole organism.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:29.971
That’s what I did
after I got my postdoctoral position,

00:04:29.971 --> 00:04:34.673
because here I work with C. elegans.

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:37.230
C. elegans is a small nematode or worm.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:41.239
And we study how they adapt
to environmental factors.

00:04:41.239 --> 00:04:45.060
They do this by changing
their gene expression.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.190
Gene expression means
that the protein

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:50.636
encoded in the RNA fragment

00:04:50.636 --> 00:04:53.883
accumulates in different amounts

00:04:53.883 --> 00:04:55.425
depending on the animal’s physiological state.

00:04:55.425 --> 00:04:58.851
In turn, the animal’s physiological state

00:04:58.851 --> 00:05:00.238
depends on environmental factors.

00:05:00.238 --> 00:05:03.586
Using the example of different animals,

00:05:03.586 --> 00:05:05.836
in particular this model organism,
the nematode C. elegans,

00:05:05.836 --> 00:05:09.459
it has been shown that certain
environmental factors,

00:05:09.459 --> 00:05:12.262
such as changes in temperature

00:05:12.262 --> 00:05:14.046
or exposure to pathogens,

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:17.812
lead to a change in this protein balance

00:05:17.812 --> 00:05:21.402
and other molecules encoded in the genome.

00:05:21.402 --> 00:05:25.511
Speaking about other molecules,
I mean those RNA molecules

00:05:25.511 --> 00:05:30.251
that don't encode protein.

00:05:30.251 --> 00:05:34.738
In addition, it was found
that these altered states

00:05:34.738 --> 00:05:37.887
of gene expression can be transmitted

00:05:37.887 --> 00:05:40.298
from one generation to the next.

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:43.067
And these things can happen for 4-6

00:05:43.067 --> 00:05:46.729
and, in some cases,
even dozens of generations.

00:05:46.729 --> 00:05:49.101
Thus, the hypothesis arose

00:05:49.101 --> 00:05:53.509
that this transmission
of gene expression states

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:56.224
caused by environmental factors

00:05:56.224 --> 00:06:00.441
can serve as an adaptation mechanism
that gives offspring an advantage,

00:06:00.441 --> 00:06:04.292
which means that they feel better

00:06:04.292 --> 00:06:06.682
and reproduce more actively.

00:06:06.682 --> 00:06:09.629
As a result, they can have more offspring
in changed environmental

00:06:09.629 --> 00:06:10.129
conditions.

00:06:10.129 --> 00:06:14.475
And this mechanism,
which lasts only

00:06:14.475 --> 00:06:17.337
for several generations,
can be considered as something

00:06:17.337 --> 00:06:21.433
between the adaptation
of an organism during its life,

00:06:21.433 --> 00:06:25.121
that is, its physiological
adaptation to the environment,

00:06:25.121 --> 00:06:27.906
and an adaptation that is caused

00:06:27.906 --> 00:06:31.878
by such factors as, for example,
DNA mutations.

00:06:31.878 --> 00:06:36.454
Once such a mutation occurs,

00:06:36.454 --> 00:06:40.629
it gets fixed,
and the organism and its offspring,

00:06:40.629 --> 00:06:42.401
roughly speaking, can't get rid of it.

00:06:42.401 --> 00:06:45.441
In the case of this epigenetic
inheritance mechanism,

00:06:45.441 --> 00:06:48.881
which is not accompanied

00:06:48.881 --> 00:06:54.093
by a change in the DNA sequence,
we can imagine a malleable state

00:06:54.093 --> 00:06:57.975
where we can inherit
something potentially useful,

00:06:57.975 --> 00:07:01.251
but if environmental factors change,

00:07:01.251 --> 00:07:04.856
we’ll be able to leave

00:07:04.856 --> 00:07:05.564
and reset this heredity.

00:07:05.564 --> 00:07:08.909
Whether this is true,
what genes are responsible for this,

00:07:08.909 --> 00:07:12.938
what mechanisms are responsible for the way
gene expression states are transmitted

00:07:12.938 --> 00:07:16.355
from one generation to the next
is, basically,

00:07:16.355 --> 00:07:19.666
the subject of our research

00:07:19.666 --> 00:07:20.639
at Professor Hunter’s laboratory.

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:23.514
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:23.514 --> 00:07:33.514
[Text:]
What is the most difficult part of your job?

00:07:33.514 --> 00:07:35.582
[Shubin:] Let me answer this question
from the point of view

00:07:35.582 --> 00:07:37.457
of someone working in the field
of fundamental biology,

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:40.169
since there are a lot of branches of biology.

00:07:40.169 --> 00:07:43.581
Also, different biologists

00:07:43.581 --> 00:07:45.339
have different goals.

00:07:45.339 --> 00:07:50.196
My goal is not to develop
some technology.

00:07:50.196 --> 00:07:54.303
My goal is not to develop

00:07:54.303 --> 00:07:55.673
some drug.

00:07:55.673 --> 00:08:01.520
My goal is to understand
how nature works,

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:03.952
that is, how certain mechanisms work,

00:08:03.952 --> 00:08:08.239
and, of course,
to choose the right experimental model,

00:08:08.239 --> 00:08:11.026
to decide which direction to take,

00:08:11.026 --> 00:08:14.301
which experiments to do,

00:08:14.301 --> 00:08:18.575
which approach to use,

00:08:18.575 --> 00:08:22.523
especially in the context of my career
as a postdoctoral researcher,

00:08:22.523 --> 00:08:26.377
given the fact that this position
is limited in time.

00:08:26.377 --> 00:08:29.269
So, we need to solve all these problems

00:08:29.269 --> 00:08:33.332
within a certain time

00:08:33.332 --> 00:08:35.567
and get a certain formal result,

00:08:35.567 --> 00:08:38.389
because if you want to move on,

00:08:38.389 --> 00:08:41.578
you have to publish your results
in such a way

00:08:41.578 --> 00:08:45.345
that your colleagues believe in them,

00:08:45.345 --> 00:08:49.401
including those colleagues
who want to see you

00:08:49.401 --> 00:08:52.506
as an assistant professor
at some university

00:08:52.506 --> 00:08:53.127
or institute.

00:08:53.127 --> 00:08:54.904
Your results should appear

00:08:54.904 --> 00:08:58.036
to your colleagues
to be really significant.

00:08:58.036 --> 00:09:00.481
It's quite difficult to do this
in fundamental biology

00:09:00.481 --> 00:09:04.143
and, probably, in any other branch of biology.

00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:06.823
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:06.823 --> 00:09:11.823
[Text:] In your opinion, what are the most
significant discoveries in the

00:09:11.823 --> 00:09:16.823
20th and 21st centuries that determined
the development of biology as a science?

00:09:16.823 --> 00:09:19.270
[Shubin:] Yes, that's a very broad question.

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:21.591
The thing is that almost everything

00:09:21.591 --> 00:09:24.868
that ever happened in biology
happened in the 20th century,

00:09:24.868 --> 00:09:27.989
since the sciences that we know,

00:09:27.989 --> 00:09:33.928
such as immunology and biochemistry,
emerged as independent disciplines.

00:09:33.928 --> 00:09:36.606
Many of those sciences
emerged in the 20th century,

00:09:36.606 --> 00:09:39.834
including molecular biology
and molecular genetics.

00:09:39.834 --> 00:09:41.887
Generally speaking,
the 20th century began

00:09:41.887 --> 00:09:43.559
with the rediscovery of Mendel's laws,

00:09:43.559 --> 00:09:46.727
which had a huge impact
on the development of biology,

00:09:46.727 --> 00:09:48.859
and this influence can hardly
be overestimated.

00:09:48.859 --> 00:09:54.571
Later,

00:09:54.571 --> 00:09:58.477
deoxyribonucleic acid was identified

00:09:58.477 --> 00:10:01.793
as a carrier of genetic

00:10:01.793 --> 00:10:03.835
information,
which, of course,

00:10:03.835 --> 00:10:04.335
was also a significant discovery.

00:10:04.335 --> 00:10:07.534
After that, gene structure
was determined,

00:10:07.534 --> 00:10:12.923
including the way it works,
the genetic code,

00:10:12.923 --> 00:10:14.084
and so on and so forth.

00:10:14.084 --> 00:10:17.532
I mean, it’s hard to say
what was the most important...

00:10:17.532 --> 00:10:20.427
Maybe this, because it helped us

00:10:20.427 --> 00:10:24.813
understand how nature works,

00:10:24.813 --> 00:10:27.306
and, of course, how we can use it,
since biotechnology emerged

00:10:27.306 --> 00:10:29.164
right after the development
of fundamental biology.

00:10:29.164 --> 00:10:33.331
Its achievements in the 20th century
are simply amazing.

00:10:33.331 --> 00:10:38.969
It’s hard to imagine

00:10:38.969 --> 00:10:41.744
how much has been done

00:10:41.744 --> 00:10:44.897
by these scientists.

00:10:44.897 --> 00:10:47.966
Even though I majored in biotechnology

00:10:47.966 --> 00:10:50.363
and have been working
in this field throughout my career,

00:10:50.363 --> 00:10:53.163
it’s impossible even for me
to imagine that.

00:10:53.163 --> 00:10:55.343
It has completely changed our lives.

00:10:55.343 --> 00:10:57.936
Speaking about the 21st century,

00:10:57.936 --> 00:11:00.501
of course, we should mention the fact

00:11:00.501 --> 00:11:03.845
that genome editing technology
has been commercialized

00:11:03.845 --> 00:11:07.067
or at least become part
of everyday laboratory work.

00:11:07.067 --> 00:11:11.160
In addition,

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:13.839
I think the development
of high-throughput sequencing methods

00:11:13.839 --> 00:11:18.408
has also had a huge impact…
it means we can now read the genomes

00:11:18.408 --> 00:11:20.034
of the organisms
we are interested in.

00:11:20.034 --> 00:11:24.708
In addition, we can read
the genomes of a single cell.

00:11:24.708 --> 00:11:28.390
Also, we can read
the transcriptomes of a single cell,

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:32.007
that is, all the RNA molecules
that can be synthesized

00:11:32.007 --> 00:11:32.731
from a genomic RNA template.

00:11:32.731 --> 00:11:36.908
As a result, our understanding
of the mechanisms

00:11:36.908 --> 00:11:42.180
of organism development
and disease development

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:46.051
has improved significantly.

00:11:46.051 --> 00:11:49.456
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:11:49.456 --> 00:11:59.456
[Text:] Could you tell us how the vaccines work?

00:11:59.456 --> 00:12:02.396
[Shubin:] I would like to explain
how BioNTech's mRNA vaccine works.

00:12:02.396 --> 00:12:04.766
Supporting information

00:12:04.766 --> 00:12:06.399
will be shown on this slide.

00:12:06.399 --> 00:12:10.182
The vaccine from BioNTech
is an mRNA vaccine,

00:12:10.182 --> 00:12:14.042
which means it doesn’t contain
the antigen itself.

00:12:14.042 --> 00:12:14.584
That is,

00:12:14.584 --> 00:12:17.584
there are no structural fragments of the virus
in this vaccine.

00:12:17.584 --> 00:12:19.455
It contains a genetic construct

00:12:19.455 --> 00:12:21.205
that encodes the spike protein
of the coronavirus,

00:12:21.205 --> 00:12:23.535
that is, the fragment of the coronavirus

00:12:23.535 --> 00:12:26.955
to which effective antibodies
are produced,

00:12:26.955 --> 00:12:28.951
including neutralizing antibodies.

00:12:28.951 --> 00:12:31.844
These antibodies are capable
of blocking the ability

00:12:31.844 --> 00:12:33.638
of the virus to infect cells.

00:12:33.638 --> 00:12:36.755
These genetic constructs
or mRNA molecules

00:12:36.755 --> 00:12:40.497
are packed in special
synthetic lipophilic particles

00:12:40.497 --> 00:12:44.774
that allow the vaccine
to be delivered to the cell.

00:12:44.774 --> 00:12:46.894
When the vaccine enters the cell,

00:12:46.894 --> 00:12:50.020
these particles open
and release it into the cell,

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:54.733
into the cytoplasm.
The RNA molecules are used

00:12:54.733 --> 00:12:57.322
by the cellular machinery
to synthesize the antigen.

00:12:57.322 --> 00:13:00.386
That is, translation occurs:
this messenger RNA

00:13:00.386 --> 00:13:03.782
is used as a template,
and antigen synthesis occurs

00:13:03.782 --> 00:13:06.771
with the help of ribosomal complexes.

00:13:06.771 --> 00:13:10.013
This antigen is then processed

00:13:10.013 --> 00:13:13.297
by special cell machinery
in order to create

00:13:13.297 --> 00:13:16.564
small fragments of it,
which then appear on the cell surface

00:13:16.564 --> 00:13:19.502
together with special molecules,

00:13:19.502 --> 00:13:20.794
histocompatibility complexes.

00:13:20.794 --> 00:13:24.292
This is where immune cells
come into play.

00:13:24.292 --> 00:13:26.731
All four of them are shown here.

00:13:26.731 --> 00:13:29.682
The T-helper recognizes
this antigen fragment

00:13:29.682 --> 00:13:32.690
as a foreign fragment

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:34.052
that can't...

00:13:34.052 --> 00:13:37.017
that was not synthesized
in the host organism.

00:13:37.017 --> 00:13:39.838
The T-helper is then activated.

00:13:39.838 --> 00:13:42.585
Cytotoxic T-killers are also activated.
These cells are able

00:13:42.585 --> 00:13:44.680
to kill other cells

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:46.996
that have the antigen on their surface,

00:13:46.996 --> 00:13:49.536
including those cells
that are infected with the virus.

00:13:49.536 --> 00:13:52.284
In addition, T-helpers activate B-cells

00:13:52.284 --> 00:13:55.799
that are responsible
for the production of antibodies.

00:13:55.799 --> 00:13:59.941
As I already mentioned,
in the case of this vaccine,

00:13:59.941 --> 00:14:02.348
these antibodies have
a pronounced neutralizing ability,

00:14:02.348 --> 00:14:05.972
that is, they can effectively prevent
the infection of cells

00:14:05.972 --> 00:14:09.529
with a virus that is in the extracellular

00:14:09.529 --> 00:14:10.122
space.

00:14:10.122 --> 00:14:12.871
Thus, since this vaccine is very effective,

00:14:12.871 --> 00:14:16.580
it can significantly reduce

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:19.755
the mortality from COVID-19.
It can also significantly reduce

00:14:19.755 --> 00:14:20.704
the risk of infection.

00:14:20.704 --> 00:14:24.291
And even if you get sick,

00:14:24.291 --> 00:14:27.125
it will probably be just a mild case.

00:14:27.125 --> 00:14:29.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:39.780
[Text:] What areas in biology do you consider
the most promising now?

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:44.234
[Shubin:] This conventional view does exist,

00:14:44.234 --> 00:14:48.065
but I think some experts

00:14:48.065 --> 00:14:49.517
would disagree with it.

00:14:49.517 --> 00:14:52.899
Neurobiology is very popular today.

00:14:52.899 --> 00:14:56.431
Indeed, it is developing
at a very fast pace.

00:14:56.431 --> 00:15:00.884
In addition, we have some subfields
of computer biology,

00:15:00.884 --> 00:15:03.601
computational biology,
and bioinformatics,

00:15:03.601 --> 00:15:06.080
but as a geneticist and molecular biologist

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:09.818
I can say that these disciplines

00:15:09.818 --> 00:15:12.508
are extremely promising,
especially in terms of the development

00:15:12.508 --> 00:15:14.523
of new technologies in healthcare,
including new drugs.

00:15:14.523 --> 00:15:19.298
Therefore, it’s hard for me
to answer this question,

00:15:19.298 --> 00:15:22.785
since modern biology has a lot of

00:15:22.785 --> 00:15:26.319
different subfields,
and all of them emerged

00:15:26.319 --> 00:15:29.961
to help us study nature
and serve humanity.

00:15:29.961 --> 00:15:33.676
All of them are developing
very rapidly,

00:15:33.676 --> 00:15:36.311
and all of them can be of great benefit to us.

00:15:36.311 --> 00:15:38.687
Thus, all of them are promising.

00:15:38.687 --> 00:15:41.967
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:15:41.967 --> 00:15:51.967
[Text:] What are some opportunities in
the field of biology for those who speak Russian?

00:15:51.967 --> 00:15:54.915
[Shubin:] It's true that English

00:15:54.915 --> 00:15:57.239
is the main language in biology,

00:15:57.239 --> 00:16:00.789
and I think the same is true
of other sciences.

00:16:00.789 --> 00:16:07.038
As for the Russian language,
it really helped me

00:16:07.038 --> 00:16:09.698
when I was interested
in the history of science.

00:16:09.698 --> 00:16:14.315
For example, it was a great surprise for me

00:16:14.315 --> 00:16:17.600
to learn that the fundamental
evolutionary ideas

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:20.612
were formulated not only
by Western scientists,

00:16:20.612 --> 00:16:23.485
but also by scientists who worked
in Russia or the Russian Empire.

00:16:23.485 --> 00:16:27.447
And, of course, it was very interesting
to read their original works

00:16:27.447 --> 00:16:29.602
and to follow their line of thought.

00:16:29.602 --> 00:16:32.624
In other words,
I think that for an English-speaking researcher

00:16:32.624 --> 00:16:36.451
who doesn’t speak Russian
it would be more difficult

00:16:36.451 --> 00:16:37.835
to obtain such information.

00:16:37.835 --> 00:16:41.126
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:16:41.126 --> 00:16:51.126
[Text:] Does knowing not only the Russian
language, but also the culture matter?

00:16:51.126 --> 00:16:53.888
[Shubin:] Of course, since modern science teams

00:16:53.888 --> 00:16:56.782
are international teams,

00:16:56.782 --> 00:17:02.743
it means that if you speak other languages
and are familiar with other cultures,

00:17:02.743 --> 00:17:06.054
it will be much easier for you

00:17:06.054 --> 00:17:09.826
to communicate and interact with your teammates,

00:17:09.826 --> 00:17:12.714
because you can understand
the context embedded in culture.

00:17:12.714 --> 00:17:17.673
As far as I am concerned,
I can say that the vast majority

00:17:17.673 --> 00:17:21.034
of my social circle
and my colleagues are Americans.

00:17:21.034 --> 00:17:23.505
However, even though
my English is quite good,

00:17:23.505 --> 00:17:27.565
when it comes to their cultural layer

00:17:27.565 --> 00:17:30.724
and cultural context,

00:17:30.724 --> 00:17:32.302
I have a hard time.

00:17:32.302 --> 00:17:35.981
Sometimes I ask my colleagues to explain

00:17:35.981 --> 00:17:37.090
what certain things mean,

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:39.586
and it turns out that they referred

00:17:39.586 --> 00:17:41.825
to some cartoon they watched
when they were kids.

00:17:41.825 --> 00:17:44.665
Needless to say, I didn’t watch those cartoons,

00:17:44.665 --> 00:17:46.701
or I watched them in Russian,

00:17:46.701 --> 00:17:48.431
so I have completely different associations.

00:17:48.431 --> 00:17:51.192
I mean, if you speak Russian,
you can read and watch things,

00:17:51.192 --> 00:17:52.759
you know the context,

00:17:52.759 --> 00:17:56.184
you can crack a good joke
to defuse a situation

00:17:56.184 --> 00:17:57.970
and become a great colleague.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:01.101
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:18:01.101 --> 00:18:11.101
[Text:] What is the word of the year in biology?

00:18:11.101 --> 00:18:15.042
[Shubin:] That’s a very broad question.

00:18:15.042 --> 00:18:17.822
It’s very difficult to say
what is the most important thing

00:18:17.822 --> 00:18:18.322
in biology.

00:18:18.322 --> 00:18:21.402
Let me name a word
that was relevant in 2020,

00:18:21.402 --> 00:18:23.558
2021 and 2022.

00:18:23.558 --> 00:18:26.955
I want to talk about the impact

00:18:26.955 --> 00:18:27.455
biology has

00:18:27.455 --> 00:18:30.614
on our daily lives,

00:18:30.614 --> 00:18:34.609
and I can say that “vaccine”
is still the word of the year.

00:18:34.609 --> 00:18:37.024
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:18:37.024 --> 00:18:47.024
[Text:] What is your opinion on
the ethical aspects of gene editing?

00:18:47.024 --> 00:18:50.835
[Shubin:] Indeed, genome editing
technology gives us

00:18:50.835 --> 00:18:54.049
new possibilities for treating diseases

00:18:54.049 --> 00:18:56.815
and, let's say,

00:18:56.815 --> 00:18:58.648
modifying organisms.

00:18:58.648 --> 00:19:02.473
As for humans,
of course, the main question

00:19:02.473 --> 00:19:06.228
is whether we could modify something

00:19:06.228 --> 00:19:08.020
in humans in order to improve them,

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:11.056
or whether we could tweak the genomes

00:19:11.056 --> 00:19:18.383
of human embryos
to improve human health,

00:19:18.383 --> 00:19:20.495
and so on and so forth.

00:19:20.495 --> 00:19:25.381
However, as far as I know,
some of those researchers

00:19:25.381 --> 00:19:27.833
that are trying to improve

00:19:27.833 --> 00:19:31.937
the genome editing technology

00:19:31.937 --> 00:19:36.059
have some doubts
whether this technology could be used,

00:19:36.059 --> 00:19:39.443
for example, to correct defects

00:19:39.443 --> 00:19:44.885
grounded in embryonic development.

00:19:44.885 --> 00:19:49.844
The main problem with this technology

00:19:49.844 --> 00:19:53.019
is the so-called off-target genome editing.

00:19:53.019 --> 00:19:56.835
It means that in addition
to the intended modifications

00:19:56.835 --> 00:19:59.773
you may also have
some unintended modifications.

00:19:59.773 --> 00:20:02.089
Since the genome is such a big thing,

00:20:02.089 --> 00:20:06.313
these modifications may go unnoticed,

00:20:06.313 --> 00:20:10.129
or they may appear to have
no bearing

00:20:10.129 --> 00:20:12.587
on the development of the organism,
but we are not sure about that.

00:20:12.587 --> 00:20:14.487
We are not sure
about the possible consequences either.

00:20:14.487 --> 00:20:18.220
Therefore, speaking about
the development of this technology,

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:21.832
the main goal at this point

00:20:21.832 --> 00:20:26.268
is to increase its accuracy.
We need to be confident

00:20:26.268 --> 00:20:28.702
that this technology

00:20:28.702 --> 00:20:31.496
will allow us to have
the intended modifications.

00:20:31.496 --> 00:20:33.685
As far as I am concerned,

00:20:33.685 --> 00:20:37.685
I think that at the current stage
of development of this technology,

00:20:37.685 --> 00:20:41.049
we can use it to treat diseases.

00:20:41.049 --> 00:20:44.817
For example, if a person
has an oncological disorder

00:20:44.817 --> 00:20:49.693
and can be cured with the help
of this technology,

00:20:49.693 --> 00:20:52.303
I think we should calculate
the risks involved.

00:20:52.303 --> 00:20:55.078
This person may want
to be cured of their oncology,

00:20:55.078 --> 00:20:59.378
even though they realize
that something

00:20:59.378 --> 00:21:01.863
may go wrong in the future.

00:21:01.863 --> 00:21:05.006
However, at least this person
may have a future,

00:21:05.006 --> 00:21:07.517
and they can make a decision

00:21:07.517 --> 00:21:10.437
based on those probabilities.

00:21:10.437 --> 00:21:17.540
As for the embryo,
besides the problem of the accuracy

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:19.675
of this technology,
there is also an ethical issue,

00:21:19.675 --> 00:21:23.806
and such ethical issues
can often be very unexpected.

00:21:23.806 --> 00:21:26.459
Let me give you an example.

00:21:26.459 --> 00:21:31.062
There are people out there who suffer

00:21:31.062 --> 00:21:31.562
from hearing loss.

00:21:31.562 --> 00:21:36.880
We’ve been living side by side
with such people for many centuries,

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:39.748
and such people have existed
for many centuries.

00:21:39.748 --> 00:21:42.345
And they have developed
their own culture,

00:21:42.345 --> 00:21:45.963
which is related
to their hearing loss.

00:21:45.963 --> 00:21:52.546
Accordingly, in modern society,

00:21:52.546 --> 00:21:57.662
some people believe
that there is no need to treat hearing loss,

00:21:57.662 --> 00:22:03.054
and people who suffer from this condition
are considered to be as normal

00:22:03.054 --> 00:22:04.464
as those who don’t have this problem.

00:22:04.464 --> 00:22:08.453
In addition, people who are told
that their kids

00:22:08.453 --> 00:22:11.837
can be cured of hearing loss
may not approve of that,

00:22:11.837 --> 00:22:16.477
because they believe that in this case their kids

00:22:16.477 --> 00:22:19.917
will not be able to continue
their cultural traditions,

00:22:19.917 --> 00:22:22.006
and, as a result, the cultural background
accumulated by those people

00:22:22.006 --> 00:22:24.551
will be lost over time.

00:22:24.551 --> 00:22:30.751
Thus, we are in an interesting situation

00:22:30.751 --> 00:22:34.046
where something that is considered
to be a disease by some people

00:22:34.046 --> 00:22:36.555
is not recognized as a disease by others,

00:22:36.555 --> 00:22:42.543
and it’s not clear whether parents
can decide

00:22:42.543 --> 00:22:47.035
for their future kid
whether to treat him of this condition or not,

00:22:47.035 --> 00:22:50.214
that is, whether to correct
this condition or not.

00:22:50.214 --> 00:22:53.234
I’m not an ethicist.

00:22:53.234 --> 00:22:59.439
For example,
this case

00:22:59.439 --> 00:23:01.959
makes me a bit confused…

00:23:01.959 --> 00:23:04.443
I mean, I don’t know
what to do in this situation.

00:23:04.443 --> 00:23:07.195
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:07.195 --> 00:23:17.195
[Text:] LIGHTNING ROUND QUESTIONS!
Books or movies?

00:23:17.195 --> 00:23:18.900
[Shubin:] Books.

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:21.241
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:21.241 --> 00:23:31.241
[Text:]
What is your favorite Russian book or movie?

00:23:31.241 --> 00:23:33.548
[Shubin:] That’s a very hard question.

00:23:33.548 --> 00:23:35.528
There are a lot of Russian books that I like.

00:23:35.528 --> 00:23:39.146
For example, all the books
written by the Strugatsky brothers.

00:23:39.146 --> 00:23:42.155
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:42.155 --> 00:23:52.155
[Text:]
What Russian scientist inspired you the most?

00:23:52.155 --> 00:23:54.104
[Shubin:] Mechnikov.

00:23:54.104 --> 00:23:56.655
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:56.655 --> 00:24:06.655
[Text:]
What are your three tips for young scientists?

00:24:06.655 --> 00:24:10.086
[Shubin:] Don’t rely on authority too much.

00:24:10.086 --> 00:24:13.031
Don’t be afraid to build
social connections

00:24:13.031 --> 00:24:15.246
because that’s extremely
important in science.

00:24:15.246 --> 00:24:19.097
And the third thing is to believe in yourself.

00:24:19.097 --> 00:24:23.548
We are capable of doing much more
than we can imagine.

00:24:23.548 --> 00:24:26.339
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:24:26.339 --> 00:24:36.339
[Text:] "Sputnik" or "Moderna"?

00:24:36.339 --> 00:24:37.221
[Shubin:] Good question.

00:24:37.221 --> 00:24:42.201
Both vaccines are very effective

00:24:42.201 --> 00:24:45.098
as shown by time and their prevalence.

00:24:45.098 --> 00:24:49.768
Those people who are choosing
between the Sputnik and Moderna vaccines

00:24:49.768 --> 00:24:54.331
should know that the Sputnik vaccine

00:24:54.331 --> 00:24:56.126
is based on adenovirus technology.

00:24:56.126 --> 00:24:59.536
It’s quite a well-known technology,
its properties are well-known too,

00:24:59.536 --> 00:25:04.512
and several other vaccines have already
been developed based on this platform,

00:25:04.512 --> 00:25:06.781
including those made
by the Gamaleya Institute,

00:25:06.781 --> 00:25:09.250
and they also proved to be effective.

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:12.096
As for the Moderna vaccine,

00:25:12.096 --> 00:25:17.070
I have no doubts about its effectiveness,

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:20.119
but this technology is the newest,
and I hope that in the future

00:25:20.119 --> 00:25:23.090
we’ll be able to...

00:25:23.090 --> 00:25:25.630
I mean, doctors will be able to recommend

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:29.743
the right vaccines depending

00:25:29.743 --> 00:25:31.564
on their patients’ health.

00:25:31.564 --> 00:25:35.963
I think this is the best approach
when it comes to choosing vaccines.

00:25:35.963 --> 00:25:38.807
Well, given the ongoing war and pandemic,

00:25:38.807 --> 00:25:41.746
I would recommend you
get those vaccines

00:25:41.746 --> 00:25:43.937
that are available in your region.

00:25:43.937 --> 00:25:52.255
[MUSIC PLAYING]

